1
00:00:00,474 --> 00:00:03,007
Sir, field report number two on the electricity

2
00:00:03,047 --> 00:00:03,506
file.

3
00:00:03,546 --> 00:00:04,124
Today:

4
00:00:04,164 --> 00:00:06,018
where electricity is made..

5
00:00:06,058 --> 00:00:07,491
and how it gets home.

6
00:00:07,531 --> 00:00:09,673
Last time we learned it's charge moving in

7
00:00:09,713 --> 00:00:10,312
a wire.

8
00:00:10,352 --> 00:00:10,971
Today:

9
00:00:11,011 --> 00:00:11,959
who moves it?

10
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:12,812
And from where?

11
00:00:12,852 --> 00:00:14,093
Strangest thing I found:

12
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:16,176
the power lighting their lamps right now..

13
00:00:16,216 --> 00:00:17,888
was made less than a second ago.

14
00:00:17,928 --> 00:00:18,799
No warehouse.

15
00:00:18,839 --> 00:00:20,957
What's made is used that instant.

16
00:00:20,997 --> 00:00:23,454
Billions of humans, each pressing switches whenever they

17
00:00:23,494 --> 00:00:23,899
like..

18
00:00:23,939 --> 00:00:26,699
and someone must supply exactly that, at that

19
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:27,257
instant.

20
00:00:27,297 --> 00:00:28,438
No more, no less.

21
00:00:28,478 --> 00:00:31,792
In eighteen thirty-one, an Englishman named Faraday found

22
00:00:31,832 --> 00:00:32,788
something simple:

23
00:00:32,828 --> 00:00:34,465
move a magnet past a wire..

24
00:00:34,505 --> 00:00:36,172
and its electrons move.

25
00:00:36,212 --> 00:00:38,326
Spin the magnet inside a coil of wire,

26
00:00:38,366 --> 00:00:39,960
and it keeps pushing the electrons.

27
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:40,368
Back..

28
00:00:40,408 --> 00:00:40,999
and forth.

29
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:41,825
That's a generator.

30
00:00:41,865 --> 00:00:43,517
So naturally, it makes AC.

31
00:00:43,557 --> 00:00:45,571
A motor is the same machine in reverse:

32
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:46,346
feed it power..

33
00:00:46,386 --> 00:00:47,034
it spins.

34
00:00:47,074 --> 00:00:49,340
The fan, the washing machine, the electric car:

35
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:50,769
generators running backwards.

36
00:00:50,809 --> 00:00:52,866
Faraday was a blacksmith's son who left school

37
00:00:52,906 --> 00:00:53,295
early.

38
00:00:53,335 --> 00:00:54,595
A bookbinder's apprentice..

39
00:00:54,635 --> 00:00:56,635
who read what he bound.

40
00:00:56,675 --> 00:00:57,787
Their greatest invention:

41
00:00:57,827 --> 00:00:58,963
a guy reading on the job.

42
00:00:59,003 --> 00:01:00,020
One question left:

43
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:01,139
who turns the magnet?

44
00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:02,800
They mount it on a giant fan called

45
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:03,415
a turbine.

46
00:01:03,455 --> 00:01:05,637
Since Faraday, most of the planet's power is

47
00:01:05,677 --> 00:01:06,728
made this same way.

48
00:01:06,768 --> 00:01:07,776
All that changes:

49
00:01:07,816 --> 00:01:09,282
what turns the turbine.

50
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:10,277
Their favorite way:

51
00:01:10,317 --> 00:01:11,077
boil water.

52
00:01:11,117 --> 00:01:12,857
Burn coal or gas under a boiler the

53
00:01:12,897 --> 00:01:13,883
size of a building.

54
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:15,323
Steam spins the turbine.

55
00:01:15,363 --> 00:01:17,010
The turbine spins the generator.

56
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:18,353
Out comes electricity.

57
00:01:18,393 --> 00:01:19,490
The nuclear plant, sir?

58
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:21,185
The most complex thing they ever built.

59
00:01:21,225 --> 00:01:22,811
They split the atom itself.

60
00:01:22,851 --> 00:01:23,747
To boil water.

61
00:01:23,787 --> 00:01:25,676
A fingertip of nuclear fuel holds about a

62
00:01:25,716 --> 00:01:27,224
ton of coal's energy.

63
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:30,645
Countries with volcanoes take steam straight from underground.

64
00:01:30,685 --> 00:01:31,704
Still steam.

65
00:01:31,744 --> 00:01:33,479
This planet is addicted to kettles.

66
00:01:33,519 --> 00:01:34,950
So kind one is steam:

67
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:36,227
coal, gas, nuclear.

68
00:01:36,267 --> 00:01:37,437
Kind two is water:

69
00:01:37,477 --> 00:01:38,946
a high dam, the water falls onto the

70
00:01:38,986 --> 00:01:39,484
turbine.

71
00:01:39,524 --> 00:01:40,867
The planet's biggest station..

72
00:01:40,907 --> 00:01:41,996
a dam in China.

73
00:01:42,215 --> 00:01:43,296
Kind three is wind:

74
00:01:43,336 --> 00:01:45,415
turbines the wind keeps turning.

75
00:01:45,455 --> 00:01:46,338
The one exception:

76
00:01:46,378 --> 00:01:47,302
the solar panel.

77
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:48,308
Nothing spins at all.

78
00:01:48,348 --> 00:01:50,331
Light knocks the electrons and moves them.

79
00:01:50,371 --> 00:01:51,795
And it makes direct current.

80
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:53,790
Here's each one's share of the planet's power

81
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:54,954
in twenty twenty-five.

82
00:01:54,994 --> 00:01:56,127
Coal still first:

83
00:01:56,167 --> 00:01:56,704
a third.

84
00:01:56,744 --> 00:01:57,554
Then gas.

85
00:01:57,594 --> 00:02:00,318
Then dams, nuclear, solar and wind.

86
00:02:00,358 --> 00:02:01,827
But that year, for the first time in

87
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:02,498
a century:

88
00:02:02,538 --> 00:02:03,756
all renewables together..

89
00:02:03,796 --> 00:02:04,626
passed coal.

90
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:05,566
By a hair.

91
00:02:06,788 --> 00:02:08,886
Solar alone was about one percent ten years

92
00:02:08,926 --> 00:02:09,274
ago.

93
00:02:09,314 --> 00:02:10,680
Now nearly nine.

94
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:11,951
Their fastest-growing source..

95
00:02:11,991 --> 00:02:13,325
is the only one that doesn't spin.

96
00:02:13,365 --> 00:02:14,945
Now let's follow the power.

97
00:02:14,985 --> 00:02:16,998
The generator makes it at about twenty thousand

98
00:02:17,038 --> 00:02:17,462
volts.

99
00:02:17,502 --> 00:02:18,173
First stop:

100
00:02:18,213 --> 00:02:20,518
a transformer lifts it to hundreds of thousands.

101
00:02:20,558 --> 00:02:22,214
A transformer has no moving parts.

102
00:02:22,254 --> 00:02:24,048
Two coils of wire around iron.

103
00:02:24,088 --> 00:02:25,398
More turns on one side..

104
00:02:25,438 --> 00:02:27,086
so the pressure comes out higher there.

105
00:02:27,126 --> 00:02:27,548
That's it.

106
00:02:27,588 --> 00:02:29,216
Then it travels on tall towers.

107
00:02:29,256 --> 00:02:30,276
Hundreds of kilometers.

108
00:02:30,316 --> 00:02:32,326
Near the city, another transformer brings it down.

109
00:02:32,366 --> 00:02:33,890
Then smaller ones on the streets.

110
00:02:33,930 --> 00:02:35,592
Until it enters the house at one-ten or

111
00:02:35,632 --> 00:02:36,426
two-forty volts.

112
00:02:36,466 --> 00:02:37,212
Depends on the country.

113
00:02:37,252 --> 00:02:39,864
Humans agreed on volts, amps and ohms.

114
00:02:39,904 --> 00:02:41,017
But the plug itself?

115
00:02:41,057 --> 00:02:42,587
About fifteen kinds.

116
00:02:42,627 --> 00:02:44,443
Every traveler carries a little piece of plastic..

117
00:02:44,483 --> 00:02:46,156
whose job is peace between two countries.

118
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:47,718
The trip takes less than a blink.

119
00:02:47,758 --> 00:02:49,523
The only thing here that's on time.

120
00:02:49,563 --> 00:02:51,576
Why raise the pressure, then lower it?

121
00:02:51,616 --> 00:02:53,307
Because current heats the wire.

122
00:02:53,347 --> 00:02:55,360
The bigger the current, the more power lost

123
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:55,913
as heat.

124
00:02:55,953 --> 00:02:57,476
The same power can be sent two ways:

125
00:02:57,516 --> 00:02:58,838
low pressure, big current..

126
00:02:58,878 --> 00:03:00,562
or high pressure, small current.

127
00:03:00,602 --> 00:03:02,238
The second loses far less.

128
00:03:02,278 --> 00:03:03,614
Like moving a thousand passengers.

129
00:03:03,654 --> 00:03:05,192
A thousand small cars jam the road and

130
00:03:05,232 --> 00:03:05,717
burn fuel.

131
00:03:05,757 --> 00:03:06,901
Or one fast train.

132
00:03:06,941 --> 00:03:07,970
High voltage is the train.

133
00:03:08,010 --> 00:03:11,052
And the transformer only works on alternating current.

134
00:03:11,092 --> 00:03:13,377
That's the real reason Tesla beat Edison.

135
00:03:13,417 --> 00:03:15,201
Even so, some is lost on the way.

136
00:03:15,241 --> 00:03:16,930
In America, about five percent.

137
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:19,446
In countries with old or weak grids, much

138
00:03:19,486 --> 00:03:19,876
more.

139
00:03:19,916 --> 00:03:21,983
And some of what's lost there isn't lost..

140
00:03:22,023 --> 00:03:22,923
it's stolen.

141
00:03:23,933 --> 00:03:25,263
A warning for command:

142
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:27,614
high-voltage lines are bare, no jacket.

143
00:03:27,654 --> 00:03:28,736
The air insulates them.

144
00:03:28,776 --> 00:03:31,000
Get close, even without touching, and the power

145
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:31,912
can jump to you.

146
00:03:31,952 --> 00:03:33,191
Humans hang a skull on them.

147
00:03:33,231 --> 00:03:34,784
They're right.

148
00:03:34,824 --> 00:03:36,472
A question their children ask:

149
00:03:36,512 --> 00:03:38,275
the bird on that bare wire..

150
00:03:38,315 --> 00:03:40,039
why doesn't it get shocked?

151
00:03:40,079 --> 00:03:41,925
Both its feet are on the same wire.

152
00:03:41,965 --> 00:03:44,567
Current flows when there's a pressure difference between

153
00:03:44,607 --> 00:03:45,437
two points.

154
00:03:45,477 --> 00:03:47,229
Both feet are at the same pressure.

155
00:03:47,269 --> 00:03:48,690
So no current goes through it.

156
00:03:48,730 --> 00:03:50,559
But if it touches another wire, or anything

157
00:03:50,599 --> 00:03:51,978
grounded, at the same moment..

158
00:03:52,018 --> 00:03:53,246
it becomes the path.

159
00:03:53,286 --> 00:03:54,714
And humans stand on the ground all the

160
00:03:54,754 --> 00:03:55,217
time.

161
00:03:55,257 --> 00:03:57,315
So that rule is for birds only.

162
00:03:57,355 --> 00:03:59,456
That's also why towers are so tall, and

163
00:03:59,496 --> 00:04:02,112
wires hang far apart and far from everything.

164
00:04:02,152 --> 00:04:03,979
The distance itself insulates.

165
00:04:04,019 --> 00:04:05,362
Back to the big problem.

166
00:04:05,402 --> 00:04:07,459
The grid can't store power.

167
00:04:07,499 --> 00:04:09,891
So what stations make must equal what people

168
00:04:09,931 --> 00:04:10,509
draw.

169
00:04:10,549 --> 00:04:11,522
Every second.

170
00:04:11,562 --> 00:04:13,175
One number tells them if the balance is

171
00:04:13,215 --> 00:04:13,712
right:

172
00:04:13,752 --> 00:04:14,528
the frequency.

173
00:04:14,568 --> 00:04:16,067
Generator turns per second.

174
00:04:16,107 --> 00:04:18,132
Fifty on most of the planet, sixty in

175
00:04:18,172 --> 00:04:18,908
North America.

176
00:04:18,948 --> 00:04:20,344
People draw more than is made?

177
00:04:20,384 --> 00:04:22,367
The generators get heavier, slow down.

178
00:04:22,407 --> 00:04:23,405
The number drops.

179
00:04:23,445 --> 00:04:25,187
Control orders a station up.

180
00:04:25,227 --> 00:04:26,078
The number comes back.

181
00:04:26,118 --> 00:04:28,265
All day long, nobody notices.

182
00:04:28,713 --> 00:04:30,601
Every grid has a control room, people at

183
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:32,195
screens twenty-four hours a day.

184
00:04:32,235 --> 00:04:33,023
Their whole job:

185
00:04:33,063 --> 00:04:35,386
keep that number at fifty.

186
00:04:35,426 --> 00:04:36,940
The planet's most important workers..

187
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,011
and nobody knows their names.

188
00:04:38,051 --> 00:04:39,332
The generators help too.

189
00:04:39,372 --> 00:04:41,450
Each is hundreds of tonnes of spinning iron.

190
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,798
If something fails, the weight keeps spinning a

191
00:04:43,838 --> 00:04:44,657
few seconds..

192
00:04:44,697 --> 00:04:46,271
buying the grid time.

193
00:04:46,311 --> 00:04:47,585
If a big station drops out and the

194
00:04:47,625 --> 00:04:48,536
number falls fast?

195
00:04:48,576 --> 00:04:50,446
The grid cuts whole districts by itself.

196
00:04:50,486 --> 00:04:51,794
It sacrifices a part..

197
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:52,835
so the whole doesn't fall.

198
00:04:52,875 --> 00:04:55,430
Their whole civilization rests on one number.

199
00:04:55,470 --> 00:04:56,928
It must stay fifty.

200
00:04:56,968 --> 00:04:57,750
Not forty-nine.

201
00:04:57,790 --> 00:04:59,228
It makes for strange moments.

202
00:04:59,268 --> 00:05:01,325
In nineteen ninety, England played Germany in the

203
00:05:01,365 --> 00:05:02,541
World Cup semi-final.

204
00:05:02,581 --> 00:05:03,753
It went to penalties.

205
00:05:03,846 --> 00:05:04,568
The match ended..

206
00:05:04,608 --> 00:05:06,649
and the whole country got up at once

207
00:05:06,689 --> 00:05:07,661
to switch on the kettle.

208
00:05:07,701 --> 00:05:10,350
Demand jumped two thousand eight hundred megawatts in

209
00:05:10,390 --> 00:05:10,962
minutes.

210
00:05:11,002 --> 00:05:12,367
Two big power stations.

211
00:05:12,407 --> 00:05:13,183
Still their record.

212
00:05:13,223 --> 00:05:15,573
Their grid engineers have studied the TV schedule

213
00:05:15,613 --> 00:05:16,380
ever since.

214
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:18,225
They know when the show ends, and have

215
00:05:18,265 --> 00:05:20,638
a station ready for that second.

216
00:05:20,678 --> 00:05:21,857
England lost the match.

217
00:05:21,897 --> 00:05:22,908
The grid won.

218
00:05:23,003 --> 00:05:24,975
Grids grew and joined up.

219
00:05:25,015 --> 00:05:25,478
Europe:

220
00:05:25,518 --> 00:05:27,627
one grid from Portugal to Turkey.

221
00:05:27,667 --> 00:05:29,604
Over four hundred million people.

222
00:05:29,644 --> 00:05:31,659
Every generator in it turns together.

223
00:05:31,699 --> 00:05:33,297
Same speed, same instant.

224
00:05:33,337 --> 00:05:34,377
Thousands of machines..

225
00:05:34,417 --> 00:05:35,268
like one machine.

226
00:05:35,308 --> 00:05:36,495
That makes it stronger:

227
00:05:36,535 --> 00:05:38,633
a station fails in one country, the neighbours

228
00:05:38,673 --> 00:05:39,126
cover.

229
00:05:39,166 --> 00:05:41,222
It also makes the neighbours' problems..

230
00:05:41,262 --> 00:05:41,862
yours.

231
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:43,623
They trade power every hour.

232
00:05:43,663 --> 00:05:45,258
A windy country sells today..

233
00:05:45,298 --> 00:05:46,639
and buys tomorrow.

234
00:05:46,679 --> 00:05:49,454
In twenty eighteen, Kosovo and Serbia argued over

235
00:05:49,494 --> 00:05:51,010
who should make up missing power.

236
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:52,889
The whole grid slowed a hair.

237
00:05:52,929 --> 00:05:53,760
Instead of fifty:

238
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,662
forty-nine point nine nine six.

239
00:05:55,702 --> 00:05:57,720
Oven clocks and alarm clocks, which count time

240
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,051
from the grid's pulse, fell six minutes behind.

241
00:06:00,091 --> 00:06:01,525
In twenty-five countries.

242
00:06:01,565 --> 00:06:02,645
A quarrel between two..

243
00:06:02,685 --> 00:06:04,240
made a whole continent late for work.

244
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,729
Finally, a scientific excuse.

245
00:06:05,769 --> 00:06:06,934
Japan is the opposite.

246
00:06:06,974 --> 00:06:09,892
In eighteen ninety-five, Tokyo bought German generators turning

247
00:06:09,932 --> 00:06:10,422
at fifty.

248
00:06:10,462 --> 00:06:12,503
The next year, Osaka bought American ones at

249
00:06:12,543 --> 00:06:13,005
sixty.

250
00:06:13,045 --> 00:06:14,721
To this day the country is split in

251
00:06:14,761 --> 00:06:17,120
two, joined only by special converter stations.

252
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:19,591
In twenty eleven, after the great earthquake, the

253
00:06:19,631 --> 00:06:22,021
east needed power and the west had extra.

254
00:06:22,061 --> 00:06:23,915
Only a trickle could cross the link.

255
00:06:23,955 --> 00:06:25,594
Tokyo had rolling blackouts.

256
00:06:25,634 --> 00:06:27,659
A purchase made over a century ago.

257
00:06:27,699 --> 00:06:29,584
Demand changes through the day.

258
00:06:29,911 --> 00:06:31,873
Low at dawn, rising in the morning, highest

259
00:06:31,913 --> 00:06:32,701
after sunset..

260
00:06:32,741 --> 00:06:35,738
when everyone gets home and switches everything on.

261
00:06:35,778 --> 00:06:37,962
So some stations run all the time, and

262
00:06:38,002 --> 00:06:40,146
others sit idle and run just two hours

263
00:06:40,186 --> 00:06:40,671
a day.

264
00:06:40,711 --> 00:06:41,328
For the peak.

265
00:06:41,368 --> 00:06:42,917
Their power is the most expensive.

266
00:06:42,957 --> 00:06:44,392
The year's top peak comes with a heat

267
00:06:44,432 --> 00:06:46,892
wave, every air conditioner at once.

268
00:06:46,932 --> 00:06:47,825
Or a cold snap.

269
00:06:47,865 --> 00:06:49,341
So the grid is built for the worst

270
00:06:49,381 --> 00:06:50,212
hour of the year..

271
00:06:50,252 --> 00:06:51,851
and half of it sits idle the rest

272
00:06:51,891 --> 00:06:52,386
of the time.

273
00:06:52,426 --> 00:06:54,985
That's why in many countries power prices change

274
00:06:55,025 --> 00:06:55,803
by the hour.

275
00:06:55,843 --> 00:06:57,145
A washing machine at midnight..

276
00:06:57,185 --> 00:06:58,808
costs less than one at sunset.

277
00:06:58,848 --> 00:07:00,340
Two words from the human dictionary.

278
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:00,980
Hertz:

279
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:01,991
turns per second.

280
00:07:02,031 --> 00:07:02,888
The grid's pulse.

281
00:07:02,928 --> 00:07:03,822
And megawatt:

282
00:07:03,862 --> 00:07:04,910
a million watts.

283
00:07:04,950 --> 00:07:06,270
Enough for hundreds of homes.

284
00:07:06,310 --> 00:07:07,599
Or one small data center.

285
00:07:07,639 --> 00:07:08,944
Report summary, sir.

286
00:07:08,984 --> 00:07:09,528
